AGS (E802 and E859), the concepts are based on general phase space density arguments and are applicable to all heavy ion systems. This mechanism seems viable because it proceeds by the exchange of soft gluons.

\[K+\bar{K}\rightarrow\phi \tag{2.3}\]

If, however, one considers that kaons are spin zero particles while the \(\phi\) has spin one, it now becomes apparent that the kaon coalescence model, although favorable when considering the magnitude of \(\alpha_{s}\), suffers a penalty due to the constraints imposed by the spin quantum number.

There is also the tantalizing mechanism mentioned in Section 2.2, namely \(gg\to s\bar{s}\). Shor [21] and Sorge [1] proposed an enhancement of \(\phi\) meson production as a direct measurement of QGP formation. Their proposals make two assumptions: the production of \(\phi\) mesons from a hadronic gas is OZI suppressed, whereas the sheer abundance of \(s\bar{s}\) pairs in a QGP will enhance the \(\phi\) yield during the hadronization of a QGP. In his paper, Shor claims the variation of the ratio \(\phi/(\rho+\omega)\) with baryon density or energy density as a measurement of QGP formation that is experimentally feasible. Unfortunately, the measurements of the \(\rho\) and \(\omega\) are extremely difficult and will not be available from the year one data.

Extracted slope parameters (\(T\)) from an exponential fit to the invariant multiplicity distribution may yield information on the dynamical evolution of the heavy ion collision. A deviation of \(T\) from the mass systematics attributed to radial flow could elucidate the production mechanism for the \(\phi\) meson as well as the dynamical properties of the \(\phi\) meson's interaction with other particles.

Sorge, _et al._ made a striking prediction using RQMD [19, 1]. Byimplementing a string fusion mechanism, they predicted that the production of \(\phi\)'s would be enhanced while the kaon production would remain unaffected.

While the \(K/\pi\) ratio gives an overall indication of the strangeness content in the final state of the collision, the \(\phi/\pi\) ratio, because of the \(s\overline{s}\) content of the \(\phi\) meson, may provide a more sensitive measure of the strangeness production in heavy ion collisions. One would naively expect that while kaon production is proportional to the phase space density of strange quarks, \(\gamma_{s}\), the dependence of the \(\phi\) meson should scale with \(\gamma_{s}^{2}\). There is currently no clear description of \(s\) and \(\overline{s}\) hadronization.

Experimentally, the \(\phi\) to \(K^{-}\) ratio is the most promising: \(K^{-}\)'s are produced only via pair production, very few resonances decay into kaons when compared to pions, and the analysis is possible with the data in hand. A comparison of the \(\phi/K^{-}\) ratio as a function of \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}\) and collision system (\(p+p\), \(p+\overline{p}\), \(A+A\)) could provide an insight to the production mechanisms for the \(\phi\) meson in heavy ion collisions. If the \(\phi/K^{-}\) ratio differs as a function of \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}\) and/or system size, this may indicate that dynamical processes such as kaon coalescence play a significant role in \(\phi\) meson production. On the other hand, if the ratio is independent of both collision energy and system size, this will put a severe constraint on the production mechanisms responsible for \(\phi\) meson production. At the very least, a similar \(\phi/K^{-}\) ratio for all energies and system sizes would indicate that the probability for strange quark hadronization into a \(\phi\) meson or \(K^{-}\) meson is a universal property. One may then study the possible production mechanisms for the \(\phi\) meson in simpler systems and possibly make a statement on the actual production process: whether this be \(ggg\to\phi\), \(s+\overline{s}\to\phi\) or \(K+\overline{K}\to\phi\).
Recent results from STAR [Adl01a] show that unlike the AGS and SPS where the stopping of projectile nucleons contributed significantly to the initial conditions of the heavy ion collision, anti-baryon and baryon pair production becomes more important. Results of the mid-rapidity anti-baryon to baryon (\(\vec{B}/B\)) ratio show that as a function of \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}\) (Figure 2.2), this ratio is approaching unity in the central rapidity region for the most central heavy ion collisions through the year 2000 RHIC energy (\(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}=130\) GeV).

The production properties of the \(\phi\) meson in this environment, when compared to different colliding systems and system energies from \(Au+Au\) collisions at the AGS to \(p+\vec{p}\) collisions at the Tevatron will be addressed. Of interest is the

Figure 2.2: Anti-baryon to baryon ratio vs. \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}\). Heavy ion data for \(\vec{\Lambda}/\Lambda\) and \(\vec{p}/p\) are shown as well as the \(\vec{p}/p\) ratio from \(p+p\) collisions. There is considerable stopping in heavy ion collisions at the AGS and SPS (\(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}=4.9\) and 17.2 GeV, respectively). Year 2000 RHIC data at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}=130\) GeV indicates that \(B-\vec{B}\) pair production accounts for over half of the baryon number in the central rapidity region.

central rapidity region at RHIC which is most likely dominated by the dynamics of gluons. A comparison of the production properties of the \(\phi\) meson in different collision environments may elucidate the dominant production mechanism for the \(\phi\) meson and its interaction with other particles. In particular, if one observes significant differences in the particle ratio \(\phi/K^{-}\) between the AGS where the net baryon number is high and RHIC where the net baryon number is low and pair production is the dominant mechanism (at least for baryons and anti-baryons, presumably for quarks and anti-quarks), one may deduce from this difference that mesons carrying strange quarks are sensitive to the initial conditions of the colliding system. Comparisons of the heavy ion data to \(p+p\) collisions will also shed light on this matter. On the other hand, the lack of a significant dependence of the \(\phi/K^{-}\) ratio on collision energy and system size would indicate that the probability of anti-strange quark hadronization into either a \(\phi\) or \(K^{-}\) meson is universal.

#### 2.3.3 Dynamical Properties of the \(\phi\) Meson

Equation 2.4 shows the expected relation between the measured slope parameter from an exponential fit to the invariant transverse mass (\(m_{t}\)) distribution, \(T\), the thermal freeze out temperature, \(T_{0}\), and the common expansion velocity, \(\beta\)[10]. This equation is approximately valid under the assumption that all particles approach the hydrodynamical limit because of large cross-sections. Section 8.2 contains a brief discussion on the origins of Equation 2.4. Any deviation from this linear trend with mass may be indicative of a quantitative difference in the dynamical evolution of these particles.

\[\langle T\rangle=T_{0}+m\beta^{2} \tag{2.4}\]In central \(Pb+Pb\) collisions at the SPS (nucleon-nucleon center of mass energy \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{NN}}}=17.2\,\mathrm{GeV}\)), the slope parameter (\(T\)) in an exponential fit to the transverse mass (\(m_{t}\)) distribution at mid-rapidity (\(\propto e^{-m_{t}/T}\)) follows a systematic trend as a function of hadron mass for pions, kaons and protons [1] (Figure 2.3). This observation is interpreted as resulting from a common expansion velocity developed in the final state for pions, kaons, and protons [10]. The slope parameters measured for the multi-strange hyperons \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\)[11], however, show deviations from a linear mass dependence, suggesting that these particles do not interact as strongly in the final state at SPS energies [12]. Measurements

